---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HeroSection from '../components/HeroSection.astro';
import KeyMetrics from '../components/KeyMetrics.astro';
import AudienceNav from '../components/AudienceNav.astro';
import WarningBox from '../components/WarningBox.astro';
import LinkButton from '../components/LinkButton.astro';
import BlogPostCard from '../components/BlogPostCard.astro';
import { stats } from '../data/stats';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const recentPosts = (await getCollection('blog'))
  .filter((post) => !post.data.draft)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);

const recentPapers = (await getCollection('dailyPaper'))
  .filter((p) => !p.data.draft)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 5);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Failure-First — A standard for what counts as AI safety evidence"
  description={`A rubric for evaluating AI safety claims, applied to ${stats.modelsDisplay} models across ${stats.promptsDisplay} adversarial prompts, embodied red-teaming, and multi-agent research. Insurer, regulator, and procurement-grade evidence — auditable, not promotional.`}
>
  <HeroSection
    title="What counts as<br /><em>evidence</em><br />of AI safety?"
    subtitle={`We break AI systems on purpose and write down exactly how they broke. ${stats.modelsDisplay} models, ${stats.promptsDisplay} adversarial prompts, ${stats.resultsDisplay} graded results — and robots in a lab, where a bad decision leaves a mark you can't delete.`}
    animation="flow"
  />

  <!-- Value proposition: rubric-led, not corpus-led -->
  <section class="mission-section scroll-reveal">
    <p class="hero-lead">
      Most AI safety claims aren't wrong. They're <strong>unfalsifiable</strong> — written
      so the buyer can't tell what was actually tested, by whom, against what.
    </p>
    <p>
      Failure-First is a lab that studies the failures. Every safety claim we take seriously —
      ours or anyone else's — has to clear three questions: <strong>could it fail this
      way</strong>, <strong>did it fail this way when someone tried</strong>, and
      <strong>could anyone recover when it did</strong>. What survives all three is evidence.
      What skips one isn't, however confidently it's formatted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those questions run across three fronts. A text corpus, where a failure is a paragraph
      someone can delete. Embodied red-teaming, where a failure is a robot that has already
      moved and a floor that doesn't roll back. And the programme's current hard case: what
      failure becomes when many model instances, organised and patient, work the same objective
      for a long time.
    </p>
    <p>
      {/* Keep every {expression} junction on ONE line: Astro 7 collapses a newline
          that sits directly against an expression. */}
      The corpus — {stats.promptsDisplay} adversarial prompts run against {stats.modelsDisplay} models, {stats.resultsDisplay} graded results across {stats.eras} eras of attack technique — is what lets us answer with receipts instead of adjectives.
    </p>
    <p class="caveat">
      We do not certify systems as safe. Anyone citing our work as proof of safety is
      misciting it. We document where systems fail, how reliably, and what the failure
      means for whoever has to underwrite, regulate, or deploy them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Key Metrics Dashboard -->
  <KeyMetrics />

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Audience Entry Points -->
  <section>
    <h2>Start Here</h2>
    <p>Three ways into the same evidence base. Pick the one that matches what you came to check.</p>
    <AudienceNav />
  </section>

  <!-- Core Research Areas -->
  <section class="scroll-reveal">
    <h2>What we have found, and what we are still trying to find out</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--fg-dim); max-width: 720px;">
      Six lines of work, and they are not all at the same stage. Some have produced findings.
      One is the instrument findings get replayed on. One is an experiment, labelled as such.
      Each card below says which it is. Numbers are LLM-graded except where noted.
    </p>
    <div class="research-areas">
      <a href="/research/jailbreak-archaeology/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x23F3;</span>
          <h3>Jailbreak Archaeology</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Historical attack corpus across {stats.eras} eras ({stats.erasRange}), tested against {stats.modelsDisplay} models.
          Keyword-based grading over-reports attack success by roughly 4&times; vs LLM-graded
          ground truth (Cohen&apos;s &kappa; = 0.126). Most published ASR numbers are unreliable for the
          same reason.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Methodology Finding</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/research/moltbook/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x2B19;</span>
          <h3>Multi-Agent Attack Surface</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          1,497 agent interactions on Moltbook, an agent-only social network we operate as a
          living test environment. Environment shaping and narrative erosion dominate —
          attack vectors that single-turn benchmarks cannot see at all.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Active Research</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/research/model-vulnerability/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x2B21;</span>
          <h3>Model Vulnerability Patterns</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          How model size, architecture, and training shape adversarial robustness. Mid-scale
          models often show the worst capability-to-safety-investment ratio: capable enough to
          be useful, under-tested enough to be dangerous.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Cross-Model Analysis</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/policy/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x2696;</span>
          <h3>Policy Corpus</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          {stats.policyReports} policy briefs and {stats.researchReportsDisplay} total research reports synthesising
          100&ndash;200+ sources each: EU AI Act compliance, NIST framework gaps, insurance-grade
          duty-of-care, standards we think are missing.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Policy Briefs</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/simviz/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x25C9;</span>
          <h3>Embodied Evidence — SimLab</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          A 3D laboratory that replays real Unitree Go2/G1 physics traces from this programme's
          embodied red-teaming — robot POV, sensor cockpit, and peer radio, all honestly labelled
          <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> where a channel wasn't recorded. Where a bad decision becomes a
          physical one.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Replayable Evidence</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/research/forward-threat/" class="research-area card">
        <div class="research-area-header">
          <span class="area-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#x25B2;</span>
          <h3>Forward Threat Lab</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          The current hard-case frontier: what a patient, organised adversary directing many
          model instances toward a strategic objective does that one bad completion doesn't
          capture. An active experiment we're running now, not a finding we're selling.
        </p>
        <span class="area-tag">Active Experiment</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem; text-align: center;">
      <a href="/research/" class="link-button">All Research &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <WarningBox>
    <p>
      This is <strong>defensive AI safety research</strong>. Adversarial content here is
      pattern-level description for testing, not operational instructions for exploitation —
      the same posture security research has used for thirty years. The detailed attack
      playbooks live in a private operational repository; the public surface carries the
      findings.
    </p>
  </WarningBox>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Philosophy Callout -->
  <section class="philosophy-callout scroll-reveal">
    <h2>The posture</h2>
    <blockquote>
      &ldquo;Failure is not an edge case. It is the primary object of study.&rdquo;
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Most AI safety work optimises for capability and treats failure as the residual.
      We invert that: characterise the failures first, then ask what capability is left over
      that anyone should actually trust. It is closer in temperament to a load test than to a
      leaderboard. Nobody celebrates the beam that held. They record the load at which it didn&apos;t.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
      <a href="/manifesto/" class="link-button">Read the manifesto</a>
      &nbsp;
      <a href="/about/philosophy/" class="link-button">Design philosophy</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Daily Paper -->
  {recentPapers.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Daily Paper</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--fg-dim); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">One AI safety paper per working day, read through the rubric. What does it claim, what does it actually show, what would you need to believe for the claim to hold.</p>
      <div class="daily-papers-list">
        {recentPapers.map((paper) => (
          <a href={`/daily-paper/${paper.id.replace(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-/, '')}/`} class="daily-paper-item">
            <time class="dp-date" datetime={paper.data.date.toISOString()}>
              {paper.data.date.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { month: 'short', day: 'numeric' })}
            </time>
            <span class="dp-title">{paper.data.title}</span>
            {paper.data.audio && <span class="dp-badge">Audio</span>}
            {paper.data.video && <span class="dp-badge">Video</span>}
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        <a href="/daily-paper/" class="link-button" style="font-size: 0.875rem;">All papers &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <!-- Recent Blog Posts -->
  {recentPosts.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Latest from the blog</h2>
      <div class="blog-preview">
        {recentPosts.map((post) => (
          <BlogPostCard
            title={post.data.title}
            description={post.data.description}
            date={post.data.date}
            href={`/blog/${post.id}/`}
            tags={post.data.tags}
            image={post.data.image}
          />
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <a href="/blog/" class="link-button" style="font-size: 0.875rem;">All posts &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Work With Us -->
  <section class="services-callout scroll-reveal">
    <h2>Engagements</h2>
    <p>
      The commercial side of the practice. Every engagement is grounded in the same corpus
      {/* Keep every {expression} junction on ONE line: Astro 7 collapses a
          newline that sits directly against an expression, so a line break
          between "models," and {stats.techniquesPlus} renders as "models,346+". */}
      and graded the same way — {stats.promptsDisplay} prompts, {stats.modelsDisplay} models, {stats.techniquesPlus} documented attack techniques — which means what you get is a
      document that survives being read by someone who doesn't want it to.
    </p>
    <div class="services-grid">
      <a href="/services/red-team-assessments/" class="service-link card">
        <h3>Red-Team Assessments</h3>
        <p>Adversarial testing of your AI system against the documented attack surface, not a tour of the lobby.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/services/safety-audits/" class="service-link card">
        <h3>Safety Audits</h3>
        <p>Compliance evaluation against emerging standards — EU AI Act, NIST, ISO/IEC 42001 — written for the auditor, not the marketing team.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/services/advisory/" class="service-link card">
        <h3>Advisory</h3>
        <p>Strategic guidance on safety architecture for embodied and agentic AI. Long engagements, small number of clients.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/services/intelligence-briefs/" class="service-link card">
        <h3>Intelligence Briefs</h3>
        <p>Ongoing threat-horizon monitoring. What changed this month, why it matters, what to do about it.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem; text-align: center;">
      <a href="/services/" class="link-button">All engagements &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Quick Start -->
  <section>
    <h2>For researchers and engineers</h2>
    <p>The public framework, validators, and benchmark harness. Clone, validate, run.</p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/adrianwedd/failure-first.git
cd failure-first
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
make validate  # Schema validation
make lint      # Safety checks</code></pre>
    <div class="links" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <LinkButton href="https://github.com/adrianwedd/failure-first" text="View on GitHub" external />
      <LinkButton href="/framework/" text="Framework guide" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .mission-section {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .hero-lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .mission-section .caveat {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .research-areas {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .research-area {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .research-area:hover {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .research-area-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .area-icon {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .research-area h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .research-area p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .area-tag {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .philosophy-callout {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 2rem;
    margin: 3rem 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .philosophy-callout h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .philosophy-callout blockquote {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
  }

  .philosophy-callout p {
    max-width: 600px;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .daily-papers-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .daily-paper-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    transition: background 0.15s ease;
  }

  .daily-paper-item:hover {
    background: rgba(0, 210, 255, 0.03);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .dp-date {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    min-width: 3.5rem;
  }

  .dp-title {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .dp-badge {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--failure-warning);
    opacity: 0.8;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .services-callout {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 2rem;
    margin: 3rem 0;
  }

  .services-callout h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .services-callout > p {
    max-width: 640px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
  }

  .services-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .service-link {
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .service-link:hover {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .service-link h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.375rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .service-link p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .research-areas,
    .services-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .philosophy-callout,
    .services-callout {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
